Heavy-quark spin symmetry suggests the existence of a slightly lighter, charged partner, $T^{\ast}_{c+}$, matching the $I(J^{\pi})=1/2(1^-)$ quantum numbers of the $X_c$ system. We investigate this $T^{\ast}_{c+}$ state, a heavy-quark-spin composite, decaying via strong and electromagnetic interactions into $D^{\ast}D\pi$. Our analysis demonstrates that these decays are facilitated by one-pion exchange (OPE) alongside contact-type interactions, with distinct isospin-breaking effects arising from both mechanisms. While the decay mode is generally suppressed, it becomes more viable when $m(\pi^0) \approx 25$ MeV. Specific widths are estimated to be $\Gamma(\pi^0)\simeq 200$ keV and $\Gamma(\pi^\pm)\simeq 750$ keV. Consequently, it is possible that LHCb has already observed this $T^{\ast}_{c+}$ state within the $\pi^0 D^{\ast\mp} D^{\mp\pm}$ channel.

